module test.claim.rung_drop_standing_partition_witness_test

import gunbc.rung_drop {
  rung_drop_roster,
  standing_rung_drops,
  cited_symbol_census,
  authored_prose_rung_drops,
  legacy_prose_identity_roster,
  every_authored_prose_row_names_its_arm,
  every_legacy_arm_is_claimed_exactly_once,
  source_root_ingest_gate_rung_drop,
  consolidated_typed_drop_identities,
  every_consolidated_typed_drop_is_rostered_once,
}

// THE DEFECT THIS WITNESS EXISTS FOR, stated as the state that must stay unwritable: DESIGN.md's
// "The ones standing today" list carried a drop whose restoration trigger had already fired.
// gunbc.design_document projected that list as an UNFILTERED map over the whole roster, and
// retirement lived only as prose inside the row's `authored` paragraph, which a projection cannot
// read. So the canonical authority -- loaded in full on every turn of every session -- asserted a
// retired drop as currently standing for six days. Two sessions independently built work on that
// premise. The repair is derivation: `standing_rung_drops` filters on the row's own modeled
// standing, so the list and the ledger now answer from one authority instead of two.

// THE SECOND CONJUNCT IS THE WHOLE VALUE OF THIS FILE AND IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH THE FIRST.
// A filter over a roster where every row is Standing excludes nothing, so the first conjunct alone
// would be permanently green BY CONSTRUCTION -- the DESIGN 4b decoration this project refuses to
// count as coverage, and an executed conjunct that discriminates nothing. Asserting that the
// roster actually holds a retired row keeps the partition non-degenerate, so this witness fails if
// the retired row is ever quietly re-marked Standing, if it is deleted from the roster instead of
// being retired in place, or if the filter stops excluding it. It goes RED in the pre-repair tree
// and RED again on the state the repair removed, which is what makes it evidence rather than
// description.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH, because the residue is named rather than left for a reader to
// discover: the standing field is AUTHORED, and nothing derives it from the paragraph beside it.
// A row whose prose announces retirement while its field still reads Standing is not detected
// here and cannot be, since gunbc.rung_drop carries `authored` byte-for-byte on purpose. This
// witness closes the projection fork -- one authority for the standing list -- and leaves the
// prose-to-field agreement as the honest open residue.

// THE JOIN IS ON IDENTITY, NOT ON A RE-DERIVED PREDICATE. Asking each row "are you standing?" a
// second time here would test this file's copy of the arm rather than the projection's, and it
// would need the coproduct -> Bool accessor gunbc.rung_drop deliberately does not carry. So the
// witness names the retired row and asks where it appears: absent from the derived list, present
// exactly once in the roster it was retired in place within.
test fn standing_list_excludes_every_retired_drop() -> Bool {
  length(xs: filter(map(standing_rung_drops(), d => d.identity), i => i == cited_symbol_census.identity)) == 0
    && length(xs: filter(map(rung_drop_roster, d => d.identity), i => i == cited_symbol_census.identity)) == 1
}

// THE PROSE ARM IS ENUMERATED, AND THIS IS THE PART OF THAT CLAIM A TEST CAN CARRY. A further prose
// row is not policed here, and this file no longer says it cannot exist: one was authored on
// another lane and admitted while this witness was being written, which is recorded at
// gunbc.rung_drop LegacyProseIdentity. What a test CAN carry is the JOIN between the two spellings
// of an identity that the enumeration introduces -- the row's `identity` field and the arm it
// carries -- and that join has a real RED, demonstrated below.
//
// BOTH DIRECTIONS, because either alone admits a real defect. Per-row agreement alone passes on an
// arm that no row claims -- a row deleted rather than split, which would shrink the debt without a
// disposition. Arm-side coverage alone passes on two rows that swapped arms. The discriminating
// RED for the first is editing one row's arm to another's; for the second, deleting a prose row
// from the roster.
test fn every_prose_declaration_agrees_with_its_closed_arm() -> Bool {
  every_authored_prose_row_names_its_arm(ds: rung_drop_roster)
    && every_legacy_arm_is_claimed_exactly_once(ds: rung_drop_roster)
}

// THE PARTITION IS NON-DEGENERATE IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. A roster of only prose rows, or only typed
// rows, would make one of the two folds above vacuous, so the counts are asserted against each
// other and against the closed arm roster rather than against a literal. The typed side is named
// by a row that was declared OUTSIDE any roster until this consolidation, which is the defect this
// change exists to remove -- if it ever leaves the roster again, this reds.
test fn the_roster_carries_both_declaration_arms() -> Bool {
  length(xs: authored_prose_rung_drops(ds: rung_drop_roster)) == length(xs: legacy_prose_identity_roster)
    && length(xs: rung_drop_roster) > length(xs: legacy_prose_identity_roster)
    && length(xs: filter(map(rung_drop_roster, d => d.identity),
                         i => i == source_root_ingest_gate_rung_drop.identity)) == 1
}

// THE TYPED SIDE IS PINNED BY NAME, WHICH NO OTHER ASSERTION IN THIS FILE DOES. Raised against this
// witness rather than found by it: the folds above are blind to the typed population -- they answer
// the same for four consolidated rows as for three -- so a row rescued by this change could have
// been lost in a later merge with every other assertion still green. That is this PR's own finding
// recurring inside its repair, which is the one place it would be least likely to be noticed.
//
// THE RED IS DELETING ANY OF THE FOUR FROM THE ROSTER, and it is a different red from the prose
// side's: losing a prose row trips `every_legacy_arm_is_claimed_exactly_once` because the arm goes
// unclaimed, and there is no arm to go unclaimed for a typed row. This assertion admits a FIFTH
// typed row without complaint, deliberately -- a new typed drop is the path this consolidation
// exists to open, and refusing it here would make the control a ratchet against its own purpose.
// THE SPECIMEN, WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE A PERMANENTLY-GREEN ASSERTION READS AS CEREMONY. This
// refused a real loss on its first genuine merge, one merge after it was added. gunbc#10118
// retired `floor_cut_heal` and taught the projection to render retirement; resolving that merge,
// git placed main's incoming rows into the region this branch had MOVED
// `spark_serving_fleet_global_configuration_drop` into, and the row was dropped. THE SURVIVING
// TEXT WAS COHERENT: no conflict marker, no syntax error, no count that looked wrong, and every
// other assertion in this file green. An identity diff of the declared set is what caught it.
// That is the whole argument for asking the question at IDENTITY grain against the AUTHORITY
// rather than reading the projection: the projection renders each row by its SUBJECT PROSE, so
// grepping it for an identity slug returns zero whether the row is missing or the instrument is
// asking a question the artifact does not answer.
//
// PER DESIGN section 4b(4) THIS DOES NOT RETIRE WHEN THE CONSOLIDATION LANDS. A climb deletes the
// redundant lower-rung PRODUCTION machinery; the discriminating RED and its positive control stay
// enrolled as the executing evidence that the higher rung is still real. Deleting this as
// scaffolding for a landed change would remove the only thing that distinguishes a guarantee that
// holds from one that was never tested.
test fn every_consolidated_typed_row_is_still_rostered() -> Bool {
  every_consolidated_typed_drop_is_rostered_once(ds: rung_drop_roster)
    && length(xs: consolidated_typed_drop_identities) == 4
    && !every_consolidated_typed_drop_is_rostered_once(ds: [])
}
